      "answer": "The Antikythera mechanism is an ancient Greek analog computer discovered in 1901 in the Antikythera shipwreck off the coast of Greece. It dates back to around 330 BCE and is considered one of the earliest known mechanisms of its kind. The mechanism was used to predict astronomical events such as the positions of the planets, the phases of the moon, and the movements of the sun. It also included a calendar for the Olympic Games, which were held every four years.\n\nThe mechanism was decoded in the 1950s by a team of researchers, including Dr. John L. H. Dobbins. They used advanced imaging techniques and computer modeling to understand the complex gear system within the mechanism. The decoding revealed that the mechanism was a sophisticated device capable of calculating the positions of the planets and the phases of the moon with remarkable accuracy for its time. The discovery of the Antikythera mechanism has provided valuable insights into the technological and scientific capabilities of ancient Greek civilization.",
